tional grazing and most  importantly a new source of water and a  special cattle feed owners also  Bevilaqua put his faith in the coal the  environmental conservationists as many  here view him the experiment paid off  now the ranch consisting three thousand  five hundred head of cattle a larger  herd or much less land and they're ready  for slaughter after just two years this  is how the cattle management works when  the grass has reached a height of about  one meter or eighty centimeters we let  the cattle into the paddock they eat the  best part of the cross the leafy part at  the top after two or three days we close  that paddock and open the gate to the  next one where the grass has reached the  optimal height I mean this hem is  motoring in the meantime the other  pasture areas can recover this system is  easier on the soil the farmers also grow  a different type of grass than is usual  for the region mombasa grass is rich in  protein but requires the grazing cattle  to be rotated regularly it's changed a  lot the entire area you see here was  totally degraded for 25 years all we did  was exploit the land until it could  hardly produce anything anymore through  the partnership with pexif we've turned  it into a flourishing garden for the  cattle and productivity has increased a  lot it's practically quadrupled the qtm  which applicable quite  the land around the springs and on the  river margins has been reforested in  some cases even more than the law  demands according to McCall water for  the cattle is now piped to the pastures  from the basin improved water quality  also helps increase yield the  rehabilitated soil can take up more co2  and the modified fodder means the cattle  produce less methane paksas business  model aims to reap both environmental  protection and profits the company helps  increase yields and in return receives a  large share of the surplus for six years  soil remediation is expensive it  requires several passes with heavy  machinery 